---
import Doc from '@/layouts/Doc.astro'
import Unreleased from '@/components/Unreleased.astro'
import Command from '@/components/Command.astro'
import TerminalCast from '@/components/TerminalCast.astro'
import { Code } from 'astro:components'
import { panelTheme } from '@/lib/shiki-theme'

/* The compose file is written here rather than read from an example program,
   because it is configuration for the reader's machine and not code that CI
   compiles. Every key in it maps to a flag on the daemon. It is shown as one
   file with no language tabs, because there is only one language it can be. */
const compose = `services:
  mcpd:
    image: ghcr.io/mcpd/mcpd:latest
    restart: unless-stopped
    ports:
      - "4317:4317"
    volumes:
      - mcpd-data:/data
    environment:
      MCPD_DATA: /data/mcpd.db
      MCPD_PROJECT: default
      # Set these before this port is reachable by anything but you.
      # MCPD_INGEST_KEYS: key-for-your-agents
      # MCPD_READ_KEYS: key-for-your-team
      # MCPD_RETENTION_DAYS: 30

volumes:
  mcpd-data:
`
---

<Doc
  title="Install"
  description="Every way to install mcpd: the Docker image, a compose file, the Helm chart, a release binary, and a build from source. Then verify it answered on /healthz."
  lede="mcpd is one binary with storage compiled in, so installing it means putting that binary somewhere and starting it. Pick whichever route below matches how you already run things."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>Docker</h2>
  <Unreleased class="not-prose my-6" />
  <p>
    The published image is the shortest route in, and the one the rest of the
    documentation assumes.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    command="docker run -p 4317:4317 -v mcpd-data:/data -e MCPD_DATA=/data/mcpd.db ghcr.io/mcpd/mcpd"
  />

  <p>
    The volume matters. mcpd writes a SQLite database at the path
    <code>MCPD_DATA</code> names, and a container without a volume takes your
    traces with it when it stops. Naming the path explicitly costs one flag and
    removes the question of where the file went.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every flag has an environment variable, so anything you can pass on the
    command line you can also set with <code>-e</code>. Pin a tag rather than
    tracking <code>latest</code> in anything you care about. The image is built
    for <code>linux/amd64</code> and <code>linux/arm64</code>, and Docker pulls
    the one that matches your machine.
  </p>

  <h2>Docker Compose</h2>
  <p>
    The same container, written down. This is the form to use when mcpd sits
    beside your application on one host.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose panel-raised mcpd-yaml my-6 overflow-hidden">
    <div class="border-b border-scribe-2 bg-panel-1 px-3.5 py-2.5">
      <span class="legend text-ink-3">compose.yaml</span>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll-x bg-well p-4 text-[0.8125rem] leading-[1.65]">
      <Code code={compose} lang="yaml" theme={panelTheme as any} />
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The three commented keys are the ones to fill in before this reaches a
    network anyone else is on. With no keys set, ingest and reads are both open,
    which is correct on your laptop and wrong everywhere else.
    <a href="/deploy" class="link">Deploy</a> covers what to set and why.
  </p>

  <h2>Kubernetes</h2>
  <p>
    Each release pushes the chart to the same registry as the image, as an OCI
    artefact, so there is no repository to add first. The chart refuses to
    install without an ingest key and a read key, so generate both first.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    command="helm install mcpd oci://ghcr.io/mcpd/charts/mcpd -n mcpd --create-namespace --set keys.ingest=$INGEST_KEY --set keys.read=$READ_KEY"
    secondary={[
      { label: 'Generate the keys', command: 'export INGEST_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32) READ_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32)' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    mcpd handles <code>SIGTERM</code> and shuts down gracefully, which is what
    Kubernetes sends. It exposes <code>/healthz</code> for liveness and
    <code>/readyz</code> for readiness, and the two are deliberately different:
    liveness never touches storage, so a slow database cannot restart the pod
    into a loop. Values, probes and the Deployment shape are on
    <a href="/deploy" class="link">Docker and Kubernetes</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>A release binary</h2>
  <p>
    Each release attaches an archive for Linux on x86_64 and arm64, static and
    built against musl so there is no libc to match, and for macOS on Apple
    silicon and Intel. A <code>SHA256SUMS</code> file and a build provenance
    attestation come with them. Unpack the archive for your machine and run
    <code>./mcpd</code>; with no arguments it starts the daemon.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no <code>cargo install mcpd</code>. Nothing is published to
    crates.io, and a crate built from the registry could not carry the
    interface, which is built with npm and compiled into the binary. With a Rust
    toolchain, build from source instead.
  </p>

  <h2>From source</h2>
  <p>
    Clone the repository and run the distribution build. It uses full link-time
    optimisation and a single codegen unit, which is slower to compile and
    produces the smaller, faster binary. Go through <code>make</code> rather
    than <code>cargo</code> directly: the interface is compiled into the binary,
    and <code>make dist</code> builds it first, which is the step a plain
    <code>cargo build</code> on a fresh clone has no way to do. That step needs
    Node and npm on the machine as well as the Rust toolchain.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    command="git clone https://github.com/mcpd/mcpd && cd mcpd && make dist"
    primaryLabel="Distribution build"
    secondary={[{ label: 'Faster build', command: 'make build' }]}
  />

  <p>
    The binary is written to <code>target/dist/mcpd</code>, or
    <code>target/release/mcpd</code> if you took the faster route. Use
    <code>make build</code> while you are working on mcpd itself and
    <code>make dist</code> for anything you intend to run.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the data goes</h2>
  <p>
    <code>--data</code> is the path to the SQLite file, and it defaults to
    <code>./mcpd.db</code> in the working directory. Parent directories are
    created for you.
  </p>
  <p>
    Passing <code>:memory:</code> keeps everything in memory and writes nothing
    to disk. That is the right choice for a test suite, for a demo on someone
    else's machine, and for any run whose prompts you would rather not leave
    behind. The store disappears with the process, which is the point.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    label="Leave nothing behind"
    command="mcpd --data :memory:"
    primaryLabel="In memory"
    secondary={[
      { label: 'Seed a sample run', command: 'mcpd demo --data :memory:' },
      { label: 'Somewhere else', command: 'mcpd --data /var/lib/mcpd/mcpd.db' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    <code>mcpd demo</code> fills the store with a realistic failing run and then
    serves, so the interface has something in it the first time you open it.
  </p>

  <h2>Check what it will do before you start it</h2>
  <p>
    <code>mcpd config</code> prints the configuration this invocation resolves
    to, then exits. Flags, environment variables and defaults all end up in one
    listing, so you can see which one won without starting anything.
  </p>

  {/* Every line the binary prints, with nothing set. `make check` compares the
      labels with the binary's own output, because this cast once stopped at
      sixteen lines after the binary had grown to twenty-nine. */}
  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="resolved configuration"
    lines={[
      { cmd: 'mcpd config' },
      { out: 'listen          0.0.0.0:4317' },
      { out: 'storage         sqlite at ./mcpd.db' },
      { out: 'config file     none' },
      { out: 'log format      text' },
      { out: 'project         default' },
      { out: 'projects        none' },
      { out: 'retention       forever' },
      { out: 'ingest auth     open', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'read auth       open', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'write auth      open', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'admin auth      open', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'execute auth    open', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'worker auth     open', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'signup          off' },
      { out: 'runs            sqlite at ./mcpd.runs.db' },
      { out: 'run settings    lease_ttl_ms 30000, reclaim_window_ms 30000, stall_window_ms 300000, idempotency_window_ms 86400000, park_ttl_ms 86400000, max_depth 8, max_step_repeats 16' },
      { out: 'identity        off' },
      { out: 'client ip       peer address' },
      { out: 'sampling        keep everything' },
      { out: 'quota           unlimited' },
      { out: 'redaction       on', tone: 'ok' },
      { out: 'redact key      built in, which is public; set --redact-key', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'cors            same-origin only' },
      { out: 'timeout         30s per request' },
      { out: 'limits          32 MiB per request, 10000 signals per batch' },
      { out: 'derived         duration and token metrics' },
      { out: 'aspects         off' },
      { out: 'monitors        none' },
      { out: 'objectives      none' },
      { out: 'judge           off' },
      { out: 'forwarding      off' },
      { out: 'relay           off: no --upstream' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Verify the install</h2>
  <p>
    <code>/healthz</code> is the answer to "is it actually running". It never
    reads from storage, so it stays fast and stays honest about the process
    rather than the disk.
  </p>

  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="verify"
    lines={[
      { cmd: 'curl -s localhost:4317/healthz' },
      {
        out: '{"status":"ok","version":"0.1.0","spec_version":"1.0.0","uptime_seconds":8,"storage":"sqlite"}',
        tone: 'ok',
      },
      { cmd: 'curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\\n" localhost:4317/readyz' },
      { out: '200', tone: 'ok' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    <code>spec_version</code> is the data-model version this build implements. A
    client can read it and refuse to talk to a server it does not understand,
    which is better than sending a batch that will be rejected field by field.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>/readyz</code> is the other half. It pings storage and answers
    <code>503</code> when the store is not reachable, so a load balancer stops
    sending reads to an instance that cannot serve them. Use
    <code>/healthz</code> for liveness and <code>/readyz</code> for readiness,
    never the same one for both.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then open <a href="http://localhost:4317" class="link" rel="noopener">
      localhost:4317</a
    > and you are looking at the interface.
  </p>

  <h2>Next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Send it a trace from your own agent:
      <a href="/docs/quickstart" class="link">Quickstart</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Learn the words the rest of the documentation uses:
      <a href="/docs/concepts" class="link">Concepts</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Every flag and environment variable in one table:
      <a href="/docs/configuration" class="link">Configuration</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Before anyone else can reach the port:
      <a href="/deploy" class="link">Docker and Kubernetes</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Doc>

<style>
  /* Shiki emits its own <pre>. Strip its box so the recessed well shows
     through, and set the data face, exactly as the language switcher does. */
  .mcpd-yaml :global(pre.astro-code),
  .mcpd-yaml :global(pre.astro-code code) {
    font-family: var(--font-data);
  }
  .mcpd-yaml :global(pre.astro-code) {
    background: transparent;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
</style>
